{"thought": "The layer rank views confirm: FFN1 compute-bound tilings dominate latency (2.84s each, 32 identical tilings) and energy. The critical path ends with norm2 (memory-bound, 57.4% memory, 42.3% communication) and res2 (communication-bound, 56.2% communication, 43.5% memory). The aggregate breakdown shows 68.4% compute time, 16.4% memory, 15.1% communication. Energy is 42.7% memory, 33.8% communication, 22.6% compute.

Key insight: The compute-bound FFN1 layers already have max compute_units (16384). The remaining compute lever is buffer_size (8192->32768) which could enable better tiling and reduce compute time. However, the critical-path tail (norm2/res2) is memory/communication-bound. The DRAM BW increase (64->128) already failed (5.5% objective worsening) because the MC increase (+6.4%) outweighed the tiny latency/energy gains. 

Let me check the execution timeline to understand the critical path better and see if there's a way to reduce the tail without increasing MC significantly. The 'other' group (norm2, res2, res1) has only 11.9M latency but sits on the critical path. Increasing buffer_size could help with tiling efficiency for FFN layers. Let me also check if changing chiplet_type from ws to os could help with the compute-bound FFN layers by changing the dataflow pattern.", "action": "tool", "tool": {"name": "build_execution_timeline", "arguments": {"include_sample_rows": true, "sample_limit": 8}}}

